@@ -389,7 +389,7 @@ How the generated SPIR-V expects the runtime to work:
 | Full-corpus pipeline build | `vk_pipeline_check <spirv dir> pipeline_cache.bin --jobs 4` on an RTX 4060 Ti (NVIDIA 595 driver): **all 26,503 shaders become real pipelines, 0 failures** (cs 2,689, ps 12,101, ms 9,282, vs 2,236, gs 195; graphics stages with generated stub partners). Compile time 40,784 s of CPU (2.84 h wall on 4 threads): cs 23,633 s (mean 8.8 s; the ray-tracing ScreenProbes/RTShadowTrace shaders take 2-19 min each), ps 11,481 s, ms 5,395 s, vs 275 s. The resulting `VkPipelineCache` is 3.81 GB; seeding a run with it makes those pipelines instant, which is what a fast first boot needs. The cache is per driver and GPU, and a shader whose SPIR-V changes compiles again (the checker resumes from the file). Neither register storage (one variable per register) nor smaller SPIR-V cut NVIDIA compile time: the big ray-tracing shaders are expensive as such, so the runtime should compile them in the background rather than block on them. |
 | `v_cndmask_b32` source modifiers | `v_cndmask_b32` only moves bits, so a VOP3 `abs` on `src0`/`src1` clears bit 31 and `neg` flips it (`abs` first) as plain bit moves (`OpBitwiseAnd 0x7FFFFFFF`, then `OpBitwiseXor 0x80000000`, `Gen.src_u_sign_mods`), never `FAbs`/`OpFNegate`: a signalling NaN stays signalling and a denormal isn't flushed. Until 2026-11-23 the sources went through `Gen.modify`, and the mini interpreter quieted them (`-v1` of `0x7F800001` gave `0xFFC00001`, not `0xFF800001`). **Not measured on hardware for this instruction**: the rule follows the same VOP3 input modifiers measured as sign-bit moves on `v_cmp_class_f32` (gfx1036, all four IEEE/denorm modes, row "Floating-point class compare" above), and LLVM's AMDGPU backend moves `fneg`/`fabs` through a select into `v_cndmask_b32_e64`'s source modifiers (`AMDGPUTargetLowering::foldFreeOpFromSelect`), which is only exact if they are sign-bit moves. Checked on the CPU and with `spirv-val` by `tests/semantic/test_cndmask_sign_mods.py` (15 words including signalling/quiet NaNs, denormals, zeros and infinities x `v`, `-v`, `\|v\|`, `-\|v\|` x both operands). |
 | Wave-uniform branches | VCC and EXEC are wave-wide registers, but one invocation holds only its own lane's bit. `s_cbranch_vccz/vccnz/execz/execnz` therefore reduce the condition across the subgroup (`OpGroupNonUniformAny`), so the whole wave takes the branch together, as on hardware. Before this, each lane decided alone. A lane could skip scalar instructions that the hardware wave executes (its SGPRs went stale for later code), and cross-lane ops or barriers inside the block saw a split wave. `v_cmp`/`v_cmp_class`/64-bit compares now write 0 for inactive lanes (hardware VOPC behaviour), so the reduction sees the true mask. GPU-checked with `runtime/test/shaders/ps_uniform_branch.asm`: lanes whose own VCC bit is 0 run the block's scalar add when their wave has any set bit. `s_cbranch_scc0/1` stay per lane; SCC is uniform as long as the SGPRs feeding it are. |
-| Compute shaders | FSSM header word **0x138 = `COMPUTE_PGM_RSRC2`**: USER_SGPR [5:1], TGID_X/Y/Z_EN [9:7], TG_SIZE_EN [10], TIDIG_COMP_CNT [12:11], LDS_SIZE [23:15] in 512-byte units. Words **0x13C/0x140/0x144 = thread-group size**. Counted over all 2,689 CS listings of 1.0.2.1 by the SGPRs each reads before writing (operand roles from `shader_meta._split_operands`, over the code `translate()` keeps; in wave64 code a lane mask printed as one SGPR covers the pair; an earlier check gave 2,281 exact): 2,540 read nothing past the user SGPRs and enabled TGIDs (2,193 up to the last of them, 342 fewer, 5 none), and the other 149 overshoot only through loop-carried registers in the straight-line check: every extra read follows a branch, and the register is written later in program order. Followed through the CFG (written on every path before the read), 22 still overshoot; each extra register is written on some other path (only loop-carried ones in 15), so these are values undefined on some path, not a missing input. Read raw, 268 overshoot: the other 119 are listings with embedded data words, decoded as instructions. 0x110, which looked similar, doesn't match. Layout: s0..user−1 = user data (push constants), then the enabled workgroup IDs (`WorkgroupId`), and v0/v1/v2 = thread ID x/y/z (`LocalInvocationId`, separate VGPRs on GFX10). Example: the 1,013 shaders with RSRC2 `0x998` have 12 user SGPRs, group ID x/y in s12/s13, and compute x = s12·32 + v0. With TG_SIZE_EN (none of the 2,689; 91 compute programs of the pipeline library, [shader_library.md](shader_library.md)) the next SGPR is the thread-group word, which the prologue writes: the number of waves in the group in bits [5:0] and this wave's index in it in bits [11:6], 0 above (Mesa `ac_nir_lower_intrinsics_to_args.c`, GFX6-10: `load_num_subgroups` and `load_subgroup_id` read those fields, and a wave64 compute shader's local invocation index is `mbcnt(~0, tg_size & 0xfc0)`; the library programs read it as `s_and_b32 sN, sTG, 0xfc0`, most of them then `v_mbcnt_lo_u32_b32`). Thread i is in wave i / W, as for `s_get_waveid_in_workgroup`. **Not GPU-verified**; CPU-checked by `tests/semantic/test_cs_tg_size.py`. GPU-checked with `runtime/test/shaders/cs_lds.asm` through `runtime/test/vk_compute_test.cpp`: 4 groups × 64 threads, LDS exchange between the two waves of a group behind `s_barrier`, store through a V# into the device-address heap, 0 mismatches. |
+| Compute shaders | FSSM header word **0x138 = `COMPUTE_PGM_RSRC2`**: USER_SGPR [5:1], TGID_X/Y/Z_EN [9:7], TG_SIZE_EN [10], TIDIG_COMP_CNT [12:11], LDS_SIZE [23:15] in 512-byte units. Words **0x13C/0x140/0x144 = thread-group size**. Counted over all 2,689 CS listings of 1.0.2.1 by the SGPRs each reads before writing (operand roles from `shader_meta._split_operands`, over the code `translate()` keeps; in wave64 code a lane mask printed as one SGPR covers the pair; an earlier check gave 2,281 exact): 2,540 read nothing past the user SGPRs and enabled TGIDs (2,193 up to the last of them, 342 fewer, 5 none), and the other 149 overshoot only through loop-carried registers in the straight-line check: every extra read follows a branch, and the register is written later in program order. Followed through the CFG (written on every path before the read), 22 still overshoot; each extra register is written on some other path (only loop-carried ones in 15), so these are values undefined on some path, not a missing input. Read raw, 268 overshoot: the other 119 are listings with embedded data words, decoded as instructions. 0x110, which looked similar, doesn't match. Layout: s0..user−1 = user data (push constants), then the enabled workgroup IDs (`WorkgroupId`), and v0/v1/v2 = thread ID x/y/z (`LocalInvocationId`, separate VGPRs on GFX10). Example: the 1,013 shaders with RSRC2 `0x998` have 12 user SGPRs, group ID x/y in s12/s13, and compute x = s12·32 + v0. With TG_SIZE_EN (none of the 2,689; 91 compute programs of the pipeline library, [shader_library.md](shader_library.md)) the next SGPR is the thread-group word, which the prologue writes: the number of waves in the group in bits [5:0] and this wave's index in it in bits [11:6], the wave index again in bits [24:20] and bit 31 set in the group's first wave (measured on gfx1036 through `tools/hw_oracle`, #6446: wave32 x 128 threads gives `80000004 00100044 00200084 003000c4`; one library program reads [24:20]; not measured on gfx1013) (Mesa `ac_nir_lower_intrinsics_to_args.c`, GFX6-10: `load_num_subgroups` and `load_subgroup_id` read those fields, and a wave64 compute shader's local invocation index is `mbcnt(~0, tg_size & 0xfc0)`; the library programs read it as `s_and_b32 sN, sTG, 0xfc0`, most of them then `v_mbcnt_lo_u32_b32`). Thread i is in wave i / W, as for `s_get_waveid_in_workgroup`. CPU-checked by `tests/semantic/test_cs_tg_size.py`, including the gfx1036 words. GPU-checked with `runtime/test/shaders/cs_lds.asm` through `runtime/test/vk_compute_test.cpp`: 4 groups × 64 threads, LDS exchange between the two waves of a group behind `s_barrier`, store through a V# into the device-address heap, 0 mismatches. |
 | Signed-byte dot products | `v_dot4_i32_i8 D, S0, S1, S2` (VOP3P) is S2 plus the sum of the four products of the sign-extended bytes of S0 and S1, modulo 2^32; `v_dot4c_i32_i8 D, S0, S1` (VOP2) adds the same sum to D. Emitted as four pairs of `OpBitFieldSExtract`, `OpIMul` and `OpIAdd`. **Confirmed on RDNA2 hardware** (gfx1036, raw kernels through `tools/hw_oracle`): 10 operand sets, both instructions and both source orders of the accumulating one, with sign-mixed bytes and sums that wrap; the measured words are the table in `tests/semantic/test_alu_dot4_i8.py`. `clamp` and `op_sel`/`op_sel_hi` are translated and checked against the same `gfx1036` hardware table: `clamp` saturates `v_dot4_i32_i8` to signed 32-bit `[-2^31, 2^31-1]` (both overflowing sets), and `op_sel`/`op_sel_hi` pick the 16-bit half of S0/S1 for byte pairs 0..1 and 2..3 (e.g. `op_sel` 2 / `op_sel_hi` 5 exchanges the 16-bit halves of S1, matching all 5 measured sets — the form of the 89 words `llvm-mc` does not decode, [fspx.md](fspx.md)); non-default S2 half selection stays refused. `neg_lo`/`neg_hi`, which `llvm-mc` accepts on `v_dot4_i32_i8` only, are not ignored (`neg_lo` flips bit 15 and `neg_hi` bit 31 of S0 or S1, and on S2 `neg_hi` clears bit 31 before `neg_lo` flips it; 5 sets under 8 forms, 45 words): any set bit stays Unsupported, while an all-zero field is the plain instruction. Counts: the 29 FSPX programs use the pair 27,271 times (`v_dot4c_i32_i8` 25,813, `v_dot4_i32_i8` 1,458), all in 13 programs; none of the game's own 26,503 shaders has either encoding (0 of the 12,791 words `llvm-mc` cannot decode there with gfx1013). |
 | Workgroup barriers | `s_barrier` (cs/ms) is one `OpControlBarrier` (workgroup scope, workgroup memory). On RDNA every `s_barrier` counts against the same workgroup barrier and a wave that has ended no longer counts, while Vulkan requires a barrier to be reached by every invocation in uniform control flow. Under `--wave64-emulate` the round loop keeps a single barrier at the top level, so this holds. **Native wave32 compute shaders run in barrier rounds** when an `s_barrier` sits anywhere but a top-level block of the structured tree (one every invocation runs): under a guard, in a loop, or in the dispatch loop (`needs_barrier_rounds`, `bar_scheduler`). That is the emulated loop without its cross-lane part: `s_barrier` ends a segment, each invocation runs its blocks until its next `s_barrier` or the end, then the whole workgroup meets at one `OpControlBarrier` (the same workgroup/acquire-release/workgroup-memory semantics) at the end of the round. A wave that is done no longer holds the others, and a per-round count of the invocations not done (three `Workgroup` words, by round mod 3) ends the loop. A wave that is done no longer holds the others, and a per-round count of the invocations not done (three `Workgroup` words, by round mod 3, or a borrowed LDS word with a large LDS: "Barrier-round count in LDS (SHADER-579)") ends the loop. Cross-lane operations stay subgroup operations, as in the dispatch loop, since a wave's lanes run the same segment. A barrier in a top-level block keeps the single `OpControlBarrier` in place. In the 1.0.2.1 corpus 43 of the 55 wave32 cs shaders with `s_barrier` have one nested (SHADER-91's count), so those 43 now run in rounds. Why: an Intel GPU does not survive the nested form. `cs_barrier_divergent.asm` (two barrier sites in different branches), `cs_barrier_early_end.asm` (the other waves end without a barrier) and `cs_barrier_loop.asm` (a barrier in a loop whose trip count differs per wave) each lose the device (`vkQueueWaitIdle` → `VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST`) on an Intel Arrow Lake Xe-LPG iGPU (Windows 32.0.101.8132), and `cs_barrier_divergent` hangs or writes nothing on a UHD 770 (101.6129, #634). With barrier rounds all three pass there and on an RTX 5070 Ti Laptop (617.42). NVIDIA passed them before too (RTX 4070 Ti on 610.88 and 621.24, RTX 5090 on Linux 595.99, RTX 5070 Ti). **Still not proven on the native wave64 path** (wave64 cs and every ms, run natively only on AMD's 64-wide subgroups): there the barrier stays where `s_barrier` is, inside a selection or loop construct in every ms and most wave64 cs shaders of the corpus. Branches are scalar, so whole waves take the same path, and RDNA's own barrier counts the way the shader expects. |
 | Barrier-round count in LDS (SHADER-579) | Barrier rounds (see "Workgroup barriers") count the invocations not done in `%x_buf`, three `Workgroup` dwords (#930). On a full 32 KiB LDS that made 32,780 B, 12 bytes over the 32,768 B `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` of the AMD Windows (RX 9070 XT) and Intel (UHD 770) drivers, so the runtime refused those pipelines (2 native wave32 cs in towade's 1.0.2.1 corpus, [finding](https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/pull/1882#issuecomment-7084298032)). Rule (`bar_counts_in_lds`): when the shader uses its LDS and the LDS array plus 12 bytes is over 16,384 B (the Vulkan minimum limit, so in practice an array of 16, 32 or 64 KiB), there is no `%x_buf`. The count borrows LDS word 0 at the end of each round (`bar_scheduler_tail`): after the round's barrier every invocation is done or waits at `s_barrier`, so no shader code runs; invocation 0 saves the word in a Function variable and clears it, barrier, every invocation not done adds 1 atomically, barrier, all read the count, barrier, invocation 0 stores the saved word back, barrier. The shader sees its LDS bit for bit as before, and the four extra barriers sit where the whole workgroup already waits, so `s_barrier`'s semantics are unchanged; the cost is four more `OpControlBarrier`s per round in those shaders only. Below the threshold `%x_buf` stays (12 bytes fit every device) with one barrier per round; a shader that never touches its LDS also keeps it (no array to borrow from, 12 B in all). A 32 KiB LDS is now exactly 32,768 B and a 64 KiB one 65,536 B (before: 65,548 B, refused or shrunk to its allocation). Not chosen: putting the count in LDS the shader never touches (unprovable with dynamic addresses), or deciding per device (the translator runs offline). Pinned by `tests/semantic/test_cs_barrier_counts_lds.py` (Workgroup bytes from the emitted SPIR-V and `lds_bytes` for 32 KiB, 64 KiB and small LDS, under a 32 KiB `--max-shared-memory`; ring shifts across 10 barriers that read word 0 and an early-ending wave in the workgroup interpreter, whose LDS starts as 0xDEADBEEF, in both wave orders). Confirmed by those CPU tests only; GPU runs on the 32 KiB devices are pending. |
